    "body": "Springer rare earth and gallium deposit. ( Image courtesy of Volta Metals. )\nBy: Staff Writer October 19, 2026\nVolta Metals (CSE: VLTA) has more than doubled the indicated rare earth resource at its Springer project in Ontario, placing it among North America’s five largest by measured and indicated resource tonnage as it advances toward an economic assessment in 2027. Shares surged.\nThe updated lifted indicated tonnage by 116% to 122.4 million tonnes grading 0.59% total rare earth oxides (TREO). A further 197.5 million tonnes grading 0.53% TREO are classified as inferred. The estimate also includes the project’s first gallium assessment 4,427 tonnes of in-situ gallium within the indicated resource, including a higher-grade zone of 14.8 million tonnes averaging 56 grams per tonne.\n“We believe Springer is shaping up as one of the few large, road-accessible sources of magnet rare earths, and potentially gallium, in North America, at a time when the market is paying a premium for supply from outside China,” Volta president and CEO Kerem Usenmez said in a statement.\nThe expansion comes as Western governments seek to reduce their dependence on China, which dominates global rare earth processing and gallium production. Both commodities are strategically valuable, with rare earths used in permanent magnets for electric vehicles and wind turbines, while gallium is essential to certain semiconductor technologies.\nVolta shares jumped 15% to 30¢ apiece on Monday in Toronto, valuing the company at $43.8 million. The stock has traded in a 12-month window of 14¢ to 40¢.\nHigh-grade expansion\nSpringer’s indicated high-grade core grew 140% to 27.6 million tonnes grading 1% TREO, with an estimated net metal revenue of C$384 ($272) per tonne, equivalent to C$10.6 billion ($7.5 billion) in gross in-situ metal value.\nThe inferred high-grade core increased ninefold to 26.7 million tonnes grading 0.94% TREO, with an estimated net metal revenue of C$333 per tonne ($236). The zone remains open for expansion.\nGallium could provide an additional revenue stream, although Volta has yet to establish whether the metal can be economically recovered. The company is conducting metallurgical studies to assess recovery methods and potential commercial viability.\nMagnet rare earths — neodymium, praseodymium, dysprosium and terbium — account for about 22.4% of the indicated resource’s total rare earth oxide content, a proportion Volta says is among the highest in North America.\nNorth American rare earth projects ranked by measured and indicated tonnage, including reserves where reported. Inferred tonnage is shown separately for context and is not used in the ranking. (S&P Global Market Intelligence database, May 2025; Springer at the Sept. 30, 2026 MRE.)\nSpringer ranks fifth among North American rare earth projects by combined measured and indicated resource tonnage, Volta said, citing S&P Global Market Intelligence data.\nHowever, the ranking measures resource size rather than economic potential and does not account for differences in grade, metallurgy, development costs or project maturity.\nDevelopment outlook\nLocated about 70 km east of Sudbury, Springer benefits from direct highway access , on-site power and nearby natural gas and rail infrastructure. The deposit’s low strip ratio could also support future mine development, although the project’s economics remain untested.\nVolta plans additional drilling to expand and refine the resource while advancing metallurgical work on rare earth and gallium recovery.\nThe company is targeting a preliminary economic assessment (PEA) in the first half of 2027, which would provide an initial evaluation of potential mining costs, processing requirements and project economics.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Rare Earths\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: Volta Metals\nRepublish this article",
